// COVERS DeephavenLexerGrammar: import, Deephaven, Table, tables, columns, columnDefinition, BOOLEAN, INT, FLOAT, DOUBLE, DECIMAL, STRING, TIMESTAMP, DATETIME, DeephavenApp, applicationName, function, ownership, Deployment, identifier, description
// COVERS DeephavenConnectionLexerGrammar: store, serverUrl, authentication
//
// Two element kinds in one ###Deephaven section -- `Deephaven` (a store of `Table`s) and
// `DeephavenApp` -- plus the `DeephavenConnection` that pairs with the store.
//
// `tables`, `columns` and `columnDefinition` are declared by the lexer but NO rule in
// DeephavenParserGrammar consumes them as fields: `tables`/`columns`/`columnDefinition` are
// the names of parser RULES, and the tokens of the same spelling appear only in the
// `unquotedIdentifier` alternation. So the one place a user can type them is inside a
// qualified name, which is exactly what the upstream identifier-inclusion test does
// (TestDeephavenGrammarParser.getParserGrammarIdentifierInclusionTestCode joins keywords
// with `::` into the store's path). They are covered here the same way.
//
// `serverUrlDefinition` is NOT covered and cannot be: the token SERVER_URL_DEFINITION is
// declared in DeephavenConnectionLexerGrammar.g4:11 but the parser rule of that name
// (DeephavenConnectionParserGrammar.g4, rule `serverUrlDefinition`) consumes SERVER_URL,
// not SERVER_URL_DEFINITION. keywords.dead_tokens() finds it independently.
//
// `appMultiplicity` is spelled `BRACKET_OPEN (STAR | INTEGER (DOT DOT ...)?) BRACKET_CLOSE`
// -- two separate DOT tokens -- but CoreLexerGrammar lexes `..` as one token, so a ranged
// multiplicity like `[0..1]` is rejected with "Unexpected token '..'". Only `[n]` and `[*]`
// are typeable in a DeephavenApp function signature.
//
// Also note the connection's `serverUrl: '...'` line carries NO trailing semicolon -- the
// rule is `SERVER_URL COLON serverUrl` with no SEMI_COLON, and adding one is a parse error.
// The url must be quoted (upstream asserts "Unexpected token 'http'" otherwise). It is
// written WITHOUT the `http://` scheme here purely for the harness: keywords.strip_noncode
// removes `//...` line comments BEFORE it removes '...' strings, so a `//` inside a quoted
// url unbalances the quotes and swallows the next few lines of real code -- which made
// `authentication` below read as uncovered.
###Deephaven
import abc::abc::*;

Deephaven fixture::deephaven::tables::columns::columnDefinition::Store
(
    Table trades
    (
        prop1: STRING,
        prop2: INT,
        prop3: BOOLEAN,
        prop4: DATETIME,
        prop5: FLOAT,
        prop6: DOUBLE,
        prop7: TIMESTAMP,
        prop8: DECIMAL(10, 2)
    )

    Table 'quoted table'
    (
        'quoted col': STRING
    )
)

DeephavenApp fixture::deephaven::MyApp
{
    applicationName: 'MyTestApp';
    function: fixture::deephaven::myFunc(String[1], Integer[*]):Any[*];
    description: 'A test app';
    ownership: Deployment { identifier: 'owner123' };
}


###Connection
DeephavenConnection fixture::deephaven::Conn
{
    store: fixture::deephaven::Store;
    serverUrl: 'dummyurl.com:12345'
    authentication: # PSK {
        psk: 'abcde';
    }#;
}
